# Reference output for the headless renderer.
#
# `--headless` renders on a fixed timestep with no terminal control, so a fixed
# seed produces the same bytes every time. Checking those bytes against a
# committed hash is what turns that property into a test with an opinion: the
# run-to-run comparison beside it only says a flight is repeatable, and a
# change that quietly alters every frame is repeatable too.
#
# Checked two ways, from one file. `cargo test --test golden` reproduces the
# bytes in process, so a renderer that has drifted fails on the machine that
# drifted it; the `headless` CI job produces the same files from a release
# binary and runs `sha256sum -c` over this, which also proves the two agree.
#
# Nine flights, sharing
#
#     --headless --frames 120 --seed 1 --size 120x36
#
# and differing in what they ask the renderer to draw:
#
#     truecolor.txt   --demo --color truecolor
#     ascii.txt       --demo --color ascii
#     ansi256.txt     --demo --color 256
#     warp.txt        --engage --throttle 1.0 --color truecolor
#     side.txt        --engage --throttle 1.0 --view side --color truecolor
#     orbit.txt       the same, --orbit 55,35,20
#     astern.txt      the same, --orbit -75,6,20
#     steer.txt       --demo --fps 10 --color truecolor
#     drift.txt       the same, --view side --orbit -60,0,0
#
# All nine moved when the sky stopped being a box in front of the camera and
# became a volume the ship is inside, and most of what this block used to say
# about them went with it. What each case was *for* had been written down in
# terms of the star band's own machinery — the fast paths that reduced to
# identities when the camera was abeam, the sign of the depth travel, the ramp
# the near wall needed, the fold that carried a trail across the edge — and
# there is no band any more, so none of those reasons survives its subject.
#
# What the spread is for now is simpler and mostly unchanged: nine flights over
# one sky, at both speeds, from five camera angles, in three colour modes, with
# and without a hand on the stick. Read it as a grid rather than as a list of
# special cases.
#
#   Colour.       `truecolor.txt` and `ansi256.txt` are the same flight in two
#                 spellings, so a change that moves one and not the other has
#                 landed in the writer rather than in the renderer. Detection is
#                 gone and nothing is handed the palette mode by default, which
#                 makes `ansi256.txt` the only guard on `quantize_256` and the
#                 palette-index path. `ascii.txt` is the third, and the only one
#                 that says nothing outside printable ASCII was emitted.
#
#   Speed.        The four `--demo` runs and the four `--engage` ones split the
#                 sky's whole dynamic range, and that split is sharper than it
#                 used to be: at impulse the stars now hold *still* — a full
#                 impulse frame differs from a parked one only by the twinkle —
#                 while at warp the exposure draws three seconds of each star's
#                 own track. A change to the exposure moves the *six* flights
#                 that light a drive and leaves the other three alone.
#
#                 That count is measured rather than asserted, and this block
#                 said five until something finally tested it. Pacing the
#                 exposure to the distance actually flown moved all four
#                 `--engage` runs and both `--fps 10` ones, whose drive lights
#                 at frame 60 of a twelve-second flight, while `truecolor.txt`,
#                 `ascii.txt` and `ansi256.txt` came out byte for byte
#                 identical: two seconds of `--demo` at 60 fps never leaves
#                 sublight, and at sublight the exposure is an exact zero.
#
#   Camera.       Four flights watch from outside and they are at four
#                 different angles, which is now a question about which sky is
#                 in front of the lens rather than about which arithmetic is
#                 switched on. `side.txt` is abeam, where `Eye::to_camera` is an
#                 exact swizzle and the hull's axis lies flat in the image
#                 plane, so it is still the shot with no vanishing point at all
#                 and still the one the engine lance's clamp cannot move.
#
#   Stick.        The four `--demo` flights steer and the four `--engage` ones
#                 hold every rate at an exact zero from the first frame to the
#                 last. That distinction reaches further than it did: the ship
#                 carries an attitude now, and the sky is projected through it,
#                 so a turn moves the sky from *both* cameras rather than only
#                 from the seat. `drift.txt` is the flight that sees it from
#                 outside.
#
#   Autopilot.    `drift.txt` shares every flag with `steer.txt` but the view,
#                 which makes the pair one flight watched from inside and from
#                 outside — the relationship `ansi256.txt` has to
#                 `truecolor.txt`, applied to a camera instead of to a colour
#                 mode. It is parked aft of the beam rather than on it so the
#                 camera *crosses* the beam mid-flight, at frame 72 with the
#                 drive lit since frame 60, which is the band the drive swaps
#                 sides over traversed as a ramp rather than sat beside.
#
#                 That angle is derived from `autopilot::CAMERA_TURN` and has to
#                 be re-derived whenever that moves: it is how far the camera
#                 walks in the seconds before the crossing should land, and the
#                 crossing has to land after the drive lights at frame 60. It
#                 was -60,0,0 at a turn every 43 seconds and -20,0,0 at every
#                 137.
#
# None of the nine names a sky, so all of them fly the default limiting
# magnitude — which is a fixed number of stars over the whole celestial sphere
# on every terminal, where `--stars` was a fixed number *drawn*. The reference
# is also still one ship deep: no case passes `--ship`, so all three flights
# with a hull in them fly the enterprise, and a fault that only some other
# outline exposes wants a property test over `models()` rather than a tenth
# flight.
#
# The hashes hold across build profiles and rustc versions but not across
# platforms: `sin`, `cos`, `exp` and `powf` come from the system maths library,
# and recycling the sky turns a one-ulp difference into a different universe.
# So they are checked on `ubuntu-latest` only, and `tests/golden.rs` is gated to
# Linux for the same reason.
#
# Regenerating: build a release binary and run the nine commands in `CLAUDE.md`,
# then put this block back. Say in the commit message which moved and why.
